I want to upgrade the existing stateful conversational context-management implementation so that state recompilation remains correct, causally consistent, durable, observable, and low-latency during high-concurrency traffic, bursty writes, hot conversations, conflicting updates, partial failures, and maintenance operations. Success means that new conversational events are durably accepted without unbounded queue growth; only affected derived state is recompiled; obsolete recompilation work is coalesced; direct user corrections and current constraints become visible immediately; concurrent updates cannot silently corrupt state; request latency remains bounded; background maintenance cannot starve foreground conversation work; and all state transitions remain versioned, idempotent, traceable, replayable, and recoverable.

Upgrade the current implementation in place. First inspect the existing repository, runtime architecture, persistence schema, message-ingestion path, context compiler, state store, worker topology, queue configuration, caching behavior, model-call path, observability configuration, deployment configuration, and test suite. Preserve existing public behavior unless this upgrade explicitly changes it. Identify all current full-state recompilation paths, unbounded queues, request-thread heavy computations, mutable shared-session structures, stale-write risks, duplicate-job risks, cache invalidation gaps, and missing latency controls. Replace those mechanisms with the complete design specified below. Do not return simplified code, pseudo-code, mock logic, placeholder handlers, dummy data, unfinished interfaces, omitted methods, or simulated integrations. Produce the entire upgrade as complete, unabridged, executable, production-ready code, including every modified and newly required source file, migration, configuration file, test, deployment manifest, environment template, and operational document.

Implement incremental recompilation as the fundamental state-update model. Do not rebuild an entire conversation graph, memory index, context package, semantic state, or derived representation after every turn. Treat every accepted event as a typed delta. Compute exactly which entities, claims, goals, constraints, causal edges, contradiction sets, discourse references, and cached context packages are affected by that delta. Update only those affected regions and their bounded dependency closure. Incremental view maintenance applies only the changes caused by updates rather than recomputing the entire derived view; use this principle for all derived conversational state.[web:32][web:33][web:41]

Define a complete StateDelta schema. Every incoming event must compile into explicit operations for entity updates, entity alias changes, claim assertions, claim retractions, claim supersessions, temporal-validity changes, goal activation changes, task updates, causal-edge additions, causal-edge removals, contradiction-cluster updates, constraint updates, open-loop updates, and cache-invalidation tags. Every operation must include a deterministic operation identifier, source event identifier, conversation identifier, tenant identifier, expected base state version, compiler version, creation timestamp, provenance references, confidence, validation status, and idempotency key.

Implement append-only event ingestion as the write source of truth. Persist every incoming user turn, assistant turn, tool output, system event, correction, document update, deletion request, and maintenance request before performing expensive compilation. Make event writes durable, ordered per conversation stream, tenant-isolated, idempotent, and retry-safe. Store a monotonically increasing conversation sequence number or equivalent ordering token. Never allow a derived-state mutation to exist without a corresponding durable source event.

Implement versioned cognitive state. Every conversation state, entity state, claim record, causal graph region, goal state, context intermediate representation, and cacheable derived artifact must include a revision number, content hash, compiler version, dependency references, provenance references, freshness timestamp, and status. Require compare-and-swap or equivalent optimistic-concurrency semantics for all state mutations. A worker must commit a mutation only if the expected base revision matches the current revision. If the revision has changed, do not overwrite state; reload or rebase onto the newest version, recompute only the necessary delta, and retry within bounded retry limits. Reject or defer work when conflict retries exceed the configured threshold.

Implement per-conversation serialized mutation ownership. Partition state by tenant identifier and conversation identifier. Route all mutable-state work for the same conversation to one logical shard owner, actor, ordered partition consumer, or equivalent single-writer coordination mechanism. Permit parallel processing across unrelated conversations and tenants. Do not rely on process-local locks as the sole correctness mechanism. Ensure ownership survives worker restarts, horizontal scaling, partition reassignment, and deployment rollouts. Persist ownership-independent ordering metadata so another worker can continue safely after failover.

Implement hot-key protection. Detect conversations, workspaces, tenants, group chats, or shared memory namespaces that generate sustained contention, excessive queue age, high conflict-retry rates, or repeated recompilation coalescing. Partition hot state into independently versioned domains such as recent discourse, entities, claims, tasks, preferences, constraints, document state, long-range memory, and cached context packages. Require atomic coordination only for mutations that genuinely span domains. Prevent a high-traffic tenant or conversation from monopolizing global workers, database connections, model-call capacity, or cache capacity.

Implement a bounded dependency graph and reverse-dependency index. Every derived state object must record the source events, claims, entities, constraints, goals, and graph nodes on which it depends. Build reverse edges so that a changed fact, retracted claim, user correction, deleted evidence item, or modified constraint invalidates only the downstream artifacts that rely on it. Do not use broad “flush all conversation context” cache invalidation. Use dependency-tagged invalidation with explicit propagation rules and a configurable maximum traversal budget. If propagation exceeds the budget, mark the affected region stale and enqueue a controlled repair job instead of executing an unbounded request-thread traversal.

Implement a dirty-region planner. Given a StateDelta, compute:
1. directly changed nodes;
2. reverse-dependent nodes;
3. required causal prerequisites;
4. invalidated caches;
5. affected contradiction clusters;
6. affected goal and constraint state;
7. the smallest bounded subgraph requiring recompilation;
8. the priority level and deadline for the work.

Represent the plan as a durable RecompilationPlan record. Store the plan identifier, conversation identifier, input event range, base version, target version, dirty-node identifiers, dependency radius, priority, reason codes, estimated token cost, estimated compute cost, deadline, status, retry count, coalescing key, and idempotency key.

Implement latest-version coalescing for recompilation work. Maintain at most one active recompilation job and one pending logical target per conversation or state shard. If additional events arrive while a recompilation job is running, persist all events but merge their dirty regions into the pending target version. Do not execute one recompilation job for every obsolete intermediate event version. Once the active job finishes, start one new job that compiles from the newest durable event sequence and current valid state version. Preserve all events; coalesce only derived-work execution. Do not coalesce operations whose externally visible effects require independent ordering, audit records, or compensation.

Implement semantic debounce for bursty traffic. Add a configurable short debounce interval for streamed user input, repeated edits, batched tool results, document chunk arrival, and rapid state updates that affect the same logical region. During the debounce window, collect deltas and produce one merged RecompilationPlan. Do not apply debounce to direct user corrections, explicit safety constraints, cancellation commands, deletion requests, authorization changes, or other high-priority operations that must become visible immediately.

Implement micro-batching where ordering permits it. Combine compatible deltas for the same conversation, shard, base-version lineage, and authorization scope into one transaction. Preserve event order. Reject batching when updates create incompatible expected versions, cross tenant boundaries, require different isolation levels, contain explicit user corrections that need immediate visibility, or introduce semantic ambiguity. Record the source-event range represented by every micro-batch.

Implement a strict fast path and asynchronous enrichment path.

The fast path must:
1. authenticate and authorize the request;
2. validate request size, schema, tenant scope, and idempotency key;
3. append the event durably;
4. apply only mandatory low-cost mutations needed for read-your-writes behavior;
5. record immediate direct corrections, current task instructions, safety constraints, cancellation state, and active output requirements;
6. create or merge a RecompilationPlan;
7. return an acknowledgment or generate the response from a consistent base snapshot plus an explicit recent-event overlay.

The fast path must not perform full graph rebuilding, broad re-embedding, duplicate resolution across all history, expensive long-range causal propagation, global normalization, full context-package reconstruction, unbounded document processing, or nonessential maintenance.

The asynchronous enrichment path must:
1. consume durable recompilation plans;
2. acquire the shard’s ordered mutation lease or partition assignment;
3. load the latest valid base version;
4. compile and validate the dirty region;
5. apply version-checked state mutations transactionally;
6. regenerate only invalidated derived artifacts;
7. rebuild relevant context packages and indexes;
8. update freshness metadata;
9. emit trace, metrics, audit records, and completion events;
10. schedule lower-priority maintenance only after interactive work is within service-level targets.

Implement a read-your-writes overlay. When a newly accepted event has not yet been fully incorporated into derived cognitive state, construct a temporary overlay containing the recent durable events and mandatory fast-path deltas. Apply this overlay to the most recent consistent compiled state when generating the immediate response. The overlay must be strictly scoped to the authenticated tenant and conversation, must preserve event ordering, must include source event identifiers, and must expire once its effects are included in a committed derived-state version. Never present the overlay as permanently compiled state. If overlay construction cannot establish a safe causal interpretation, return an explicit pending or clarification status rather than inventing state.

Implement staleness classes and freshness service-level objectives. Define state classes including:
- immediate: direct user corrections, deletion requests, authorization changes, active system constraints, current task constraints, cancellation state;
- interactive: recent claims, referenced entities, active goals, current open loops, task progress, response-critical contradictions;
- deferred: embeddings, duplicate cleanup, low-priority aliases, historical topic clustering, long-range graph normalization, archival compaction;
- maintenance: full consistency audits, compiler-version migrations, graph rewrites, historical re-ranking, and replay validation.

Set configurable maximum freshness lag for each class. Block or qualify an answer if mandatory immediate or interactive state is not current. Permit deferred state to lag only when the response remains truthful without it. Include freshness and state-version metadata in the context package and internal response audit.

Implement request deadlines and latency budgets. At the beginning of every request, create a deadline budget that covers authentication, durable write, fast-path compilation, required verification, model invocation, response assembly, and response transmission. Reserve a portion of the budget for response generation. Cancel or defer noncritical work when the remaining budget falls below configured thresholds. If full interactive recompilation cannot complete within the request deadline, respond using the last consistent snapshot plus the read-your-writes overlay and a bounded set of response-critical deltas. Do not wait indefinitely for maintenance or stale asynchronous work.

Implement priority-aware scheduling. Create separate bounded queues or logical priority lanes for:
1. immediate correctness and safety work;
2. interactive context recompilation;
3. response generation;
4. verification and tool grounding;
5. deferred indexing and embedding work;
6. maintenance and renormalization;
7. replay, export, and bulk migration jobs.

Ensure high-priority foreground work cannot be starved by maintenance. Ensure low-priority work cannot consume reserved concurrency for interactive response paths. Use weighted fair scheduling across tenants and conversation shards. Add per-tenant, per-conversation, per-worker, and global concurrency limits. Apply quotas and admission control before expensive work enters a queue.

Implement bounded queues and complete backpressure behavior. Every queue must have a finite capacity, maximum age, priority policy, retry policy, dead-letter policy, and overflow behavior. Backpressure is required because producer rates that exceed consumer capacity otherwise create growing queues, resource exhaustion, and latency spikes.[web:43][web:26] Define exactly what happens when each queue is full:
- persist durable conversational events whenever possible;
- reject or delay nonessential synchronous work;
- coalesce superseded recompilation jobs;
- defer optional maintenance;
- rate-limit abusive or overloaded clients;
- return retry metadata where appropriate;
- preserve immediate correctness operations;
- never silently discard source events;
- permit dropping only explicitly disposable telemetry or superseded derived-work triggers, never durable user evidence.

Implement adaptive concurrency control. Dynamically adjust worker concurrency based on queue age, queue depth, consumer lag, CPU saturation, memory pressure, database connection utilization, lock or conflict rates, model-call latency, error rate, and downstream rate limits. Do not scale concurrency solely because a queue is long; a hot shard may worsen under parallel execution. Cap concurrent model calls, graph mutations, database transactions, and context-package builds independently. Use circuit breakers, exponential backoff with jitter, and bounded retries for failing downstream dependencies.

Implement fairness controls. Enforce tenant quotas, weighted fair queueing, per-tenant rate limits, per-conversation rate limits, and a maximum work budget for any single hot shard. Prevent one tenant, group conversation, document import, or bulk update from consuming all compiler workers. Reserve a configurable share of capacity for interactive user requests. Make overload behavior visible through response status, retry-after values, queue age, and internal telemetry.

Implement a versioned Context Intermediate Representation cache. Cache only fully validated context packages. Key each cache entry by tenant, authorization scope, conversation identifier, state version, query fingerprint, output constraints, model configuration, tool policy, token budget, language, and compiler version. Store dependency tags and source version ranges with every entry. Invalidate only entries whose declared dependencies changed. Do not serve a cache entry if its authorization scope, state version, freshness requirements, compiler version, model policy, or required source evidence no longer matches.

Implement progressive context enrichment. Build an initial context package from high-confidence, response-critical state. If deeper historical analysis is needed, fetch and compile additional bounded state regions in a background or tool-mediated stage. Do not delay the initial response for optional long-range memory analysis unless the task explicitly requires it. Ensure every later enrichment respects the same tenant isolation, provenance, token budget, and version checks as the initial context.

Implement speculative compilation carefully. For streaming input, begin cancellable low-cost parsing and extraction only for stable partial segments. Do not commit speculative state mutations until final input completion and validation. Associate speculative work with the stream identifier and final content hash. Cancel or invalidate all speculative work if the final message diverges from the speculative prefix beyond the configured threshold. Never let partial text create permanent claims, constraints, or user preferences.

Implement repair and replay. If a worker crashes, a state transaction fails, a compiler version changes, a dependency traversal exceeds limits, or a state inconsistency is detected, repair only the affected conversation shard or state region by replaying its durable source-event range through a pinned compiler version. Do not require rebuilding the entire tenant corpus for a local corruption or upgrade. Make replays deterministic where the underlying model configuration permits it; record all model identifiers, prompt versions, extraction schemas, randomness controls, and compiler versions needed for audit and comparison.

Implement controlled maintenance. Move duplicate detection, graph normalization, low-priority edge rescoring, embedding refresh, archival compaction, schema migration, and historical recomputation into explicitly budgeted background jobs. Require maintenance jobs to yield to interactive traffic. Restrict each job to a bounded state region, bounded event range, bounded token budget, bounded runtime, and resumable checkpoint. Make every maintenance mutation versioned, reversible, provenance-preserving, and validated against replay tests. Never let maintenance silently alter evidence or overwrite user corrections.

Implement observability for latency and correctness. Emit structured logs, metrics, traces, and audit events for:
- durable event-ingestion latency;
- fast-path latency;
- asynchronous recompilation latency;
- end-to-end response latency;
- queue depth;
- queue age;
- consumer lag;
- per-priority-lane utilization;
- per-tenant and per-conversation load;
- coalesced-job count;
- obsolete-job cancellation count;
- dirty-subgraph size;
- dependency traversal size;
- cache hit and invalidation rates;
- optimistic-concurrency conflict rate;
- retry count;
- stale-write rejection count;
- model-call latency and failure rate;
- database transaction latency;
- context-build token cost;
- freshness lag by state class;
- read-your-writes overlay usage;
- repair and replay frequency;
- dead-letter count;
- dropped noncritical work count;
- state consistency violations;
- unsupported-claim rate after answer generation.

Define alerts for queue-age growth, breach of interactive freshness objectives, elevated write conflicts, repeated shard ownership failures, excessive coalescing on a hot key, database saturation, model service degradation, rising dead-letter count, and increasing p95 or p99 request latency. Use queue age and end-to-end lag as primary user-impact indicators, not queue depth alone; queue growth increases waiting time when service capacity is insufficient.[web:20][web:26]

Implement complete failure handling. If event persistence fails, return failure and do not claim acceptance. If the asynchronous compiler fails after persistence, retain the durable event, mark the derived state as pending or stale, schedule a bounded retry, and preserve the last valid compiled version. If a state mutation conflicts, rebase against the latest state or defer to the shard owner; never overwrite silently. If model extraction fails, preserve the event and use only safe fast-path state updates. If a downstream store is unavailable, activate the configured circuit breaker, avoid request-thread retry storms, return an accurate retryable status where necessary, and continue only with state whose consistency can be guaranteed.

Implement privacy, authorization, and deletion correctness. Do not allow coalescing, caching, overlays, or background jobs to cross authorization or tenant boundaries. Propagate deletion requests through evidence, derived state, reverse dependencies, indexes, caches, overlays, queued jobs, replay checkpoints, and exported artifacts according to retention policy. Cancel queued recompilation work that depends on deleted evidence. Mark dependent compiled state invalid and rebuild it from surviving evidence where required. Do not serve cached or overlay state derived from deleted or unauthorized evidence.

Implement complete test coverage. Add unit tests for state delta construction, idempotency, version checks, optimistic concurrency conflicts, rebasing, dirty-region selection, reverse-dependency invalidation, semantic debounce, micro-batching, coalescing, queue overflow handling, priority scheduling, fairness, hot-key detection, overlay application, overlay expiration, cache-key correctness, cache invalidation, token-budget behavior, staleness-class enforcement, deadline cancellation, speculative-work cancellation, repair replay, deletion propagation, and maintenance yielding.

Add integration tests for concurrent turns in the same conversation, concurrent turns across many conversations, hot-tenant saturation, repeated duplicate submissions, worker failure during compilation, database transaction failure, model-call timeout, queue saturation, shard reassignment, deployment rollout, cache invalidation after corrections, immediate visibility of user corrections, degraded response from snapshot plus overlay, event replay, state repair, and deletion while recompilation is pending.

Add load and soak tests that simulate sustained concurrent conversations, burst arrivals, streaming input, repeated corrections, large document events, asymmetric hot keys, partial dependency failures, and maintenance running during foreground traffic. Define measurable pass criteria:
- no lost durable events;
- no duplicate committed state transitions for the same idempotency key;
- no silent stale-write overwrite;
- no unbounded queue growth;
- bounded p95 and p99 fast-path latency under the configured target load;
- bounded interactive state freshness lag;
- correct event order within a conversation;
- foreground work continues while maintenance is active;
- cache entries are never served across tenants or after invalidation;
- corrections become visible within the immediate-state objective;
- replay reconstructs the same validated state from retained evidence;
- all failed jobs are recoverable, observable, and either retried, dead-lettered, or explicitly canceled.

Update the deployment architecture. Configure durable event storage, transactional state storage, graph or dependency indexing, bounded message queues, worker pools, cache storage, observability collectors, autoscaling policies, secrets management, health checks, readiness checks, graceful shutdown, worker-drain behavior, and rolling-deployment compatibility. Ensure consumers checkpoint only after successful durable state transitions. Ensure the transactional outbox or equivalent mechanism prevents lost work between database commit and queue publication. Ensure workers can resume safely after restart without duplicated state mutation.

Add configuration for:
MAX_FAST_PATH_LATENCY_MS
MAX_INTERACTIVE_FRESHNESS_LAG_MS
MAX_DEFERRED_FRESHNESS_LAG_MS
RECOMPILATION_DEBOUNCE_MS
MAX_ACTIVE_RECOMPILATIONS_PER_SHARD
MAX_PENDING_RECOMPILATIONS_PER_SHARD
MAX_DIRTY_SUBGRAPH_NODES
MAX_DEPENDENCY_TRAVERSAL_NODES
MAX_REBASE_RETRIES
MAX_QUEUE_DEPTH_PER_PRIORITY
MAX_QUEUE_AGE_MS_PER_PRIORITY
MAX_TENANT_CONCURRENCY
MAX_CONVERSATION_CONCURRENCY
MAX_MODEL_CONCURRENCY
MAX_DATABASE_TRANSACTION_CONCURRENCY
MAX_MAINTENANCE_CONCURRENCY
CACHE_TTL_SECONDS
CACHE_MAX_STALENESS_MS
HOT_KEY_QUEUE_AGE_THRESHOLD_MS
HOT_KEY_CONFLICT_RATE_THRESHOLD
OVERLAY_MAX_EVENTS
OVERLAY_MAX_TOKENS
OVERLAY_TTL_MS
CIR_TOKEN_BUDGET
LOAD_SHEDDING_POLICY
RETRY_BACKOFF_BASE_MS
RETRY_BACKOFF_MAX_MS
CIRCUIT_BREAKER_FAILURE_THRESHOLD
CIRCUIT_BREAKER_RESET_MS
DEAD_LETTER_RETENTION_DAYS
METRICS_EXPORT_ENDPOINT
TRACE_EXPORT_ENDPOINT

Document the final architecture, concurrency model, event ordering rules, versioning model, idempotency behavior, coalescing behavior, overlay consistency guarantees, cache invalidation strategy, staleness guarantees, failure semantics, overload policy, repair process, deletion propagation, operational runbook, dashboards, alert thresholds, capacity-planning model, and test procedure. Include exact commands for migrations, local startup, integration testing, load testing, controlled failure testing, replay, state repair, queue inspection, cache inspection, and safe deployment rollback.

Complete the upgrade only when all existing behavior is preserved or intentionally migrated, all newly introduced interfaces are implemented end to end, every required configuration value is documented, all tests pass, the load-test pass criteria are met, and no request path depends on full synchronous state recompilation.
