```text
I want to build a production-grade observability, benchmarking, and performance-analysis system for a stateful streaming platform so that engineering teams can accurately measure, explain, optimize, and enforce service-level objectives for end-to-end latency, operator latency, throughput, state growth, checkpoint overhead, watermark lag, queueing delay, backpressure, correctness, recovery, and resource efficiency under normal, bursty, skewed, delayed, and failure-prone workloads.

Build the entire system from scratch. Deliver the complete repository in full, unabridged, executable, production-ready code. Include every source file, dependency manifest, lockfile, schema, database migration, configuration file, infrastructure definition, container definition, deployment manifest, API specification, dashboard definition, alert rule, test, benchmark workload, load-test harness, documentation file, and operational runbook. Do not provide pseudocode, simplified logic, placeholder implementations, dummy data, mock integrations, incomplete methods, omitted files, or simulated behavior. Implement all integrations end to end.

Design the system to instrument any stateful streaming pipeline that ingests ordered or partially ordered events, applies stateless and stateful transformations, maintains keyed or windowed state, performs joins, aggregations, deduplication, event-time processing, watermark advancement, checkpointing, replay, sink delivery, and recovery. The system must support multiple pipeline instances, operators, partitions, workers, tenants, jobs, source connectors, and sinks. Do not depend on a specific named streaming engine. Use an adapter interface so that any compatible runtime can report telemetry through a common protocol.

Define latency and throughput precisely. Implement separate metrics and data models for:
1. ingestion latency: source observation time to durable acceptance time;
2. queue latency: durable acceptance time to consumer start time;
3. processing latency: consumer start time to operator completion time;
4. operator latency: input record arrival at an operator to output record emission;
5. state-access latency: time spent reading, updating, compacting, evicting, and checkpointing state;
6. checkpoint latency: checkpoint initiation to durable completion;
7. sink latency: output emission to durable sink acknowledgment;
8. end-to-end processing-time latency: source ingestion timestamp to final successful sink acknowledgment;
9. event-time latency: current processing time minus event time or output watermark time;
10. watermark lag: wall-clock processing time minus the currently advanced event-time watermark;
11. recovery latency: fault detection to healthy processing resumption;
12. state-recovery latency: checkpoint restoration and replay time;
13. control-plane latency: configuration or scaling change request to effective application;
14. user-visible latency: externally observable request or result latency where the stream powers an interactive product.

Define throughput separately for:
1. accepted input records per second;
2. accepted input bytes per second;
3. consumed records per second;
4. processed records per second;
5. emitted output records per second;
6. emitted output bytes per second;
7. committed sink records per second;
8. records per second per source, operator, worker, partition, tenant, pipeline, and sink;
9. effective throughput after filtering, deduplication, rejection, late-event handling, dead-letter routing, and retry handling;
10. replay throughput;
11. checkpoint throughput in state bytes persisted per second;
12. state mutation throughput;
13. context or state-compilation throughput where the platform manages conversational or semantic state.

Measure latency as distributions, never averages alone. Record count, minimum, maximum, arithmetic mean, standard deviation, p50, p75, p90, p95, p99, p99.9, and configurable histogram buckets for every latency dimension. Use monotonic clocks for elapsed-duration measurement. Preserve original event timestamps separately from local processing timestamps. Handle clock skew across distributed workers by recording clock-source metadata, synchronization status, and trace correlation identifiers. Do not calculate cross-host wall-clock durations without clock-synchronization safeguards or a documented uncertainty label.

Implement a canonical telemetry envelope for every event lifecycle transition. Every telemetry record must include:
- event identifier;
- parent event identifier when derived;
- trace identifier;
- span identifier;
- pipeline identifier;
- pipeline version;
- tenant identifier;
- source identifier;
- source partition;
- source offset or sequence number;
- worker identifier;
- worker zone or region;
- operator identifier;
- operator type;
- state key hash or privacy-safe key bucket;
- event-time timestamp;
- ingestion timestamp;
- dequeue timestamp;
- operator-start timestamp;
- operator-end timestamp;
- state-read duration;
- state-write duration;
- serialization duration;
- network duration when measurable;
- queue wait duration;
- checkpoint association;
- watermark value;
- output identifier;
- sink acknowledgment timestamp;
- outcome status;
- retry count;
- deduplication status;
- late-event status;
- error classification;
- schema version.

Do not store raw personally identifiable content in performance telemetry. Use opaque identifiers, hashes, bucketed cardinality-safe labels, or separately protected diagnostics references. Implement configurable sampling policies that preserve statistically valid tail-latency measurement and never silently discard errors, checkpoint failures, state inconsistencies, dead-letter events, or service-level objective violations.

Implement instrumentation libraries for source adapters, queue adapters, operator wrappers, state-store wrappers, checkpoint coordinators, watermark coordinators, sink adapters, retry handlers, and recovery controllers. The instrumentation library must expose a stable API for:
- record accepted;
- record queued;
- record dequeued;
- operator started;
- operator completed;
- state accessed;
- state mutation committed;
- watermark advanced;
- checkpoint started;
- checkpoint completed;
- checkpoint failed;
- output emitted;
- sink committed;
- record retried;
- record deduplicated;
- record routed as late;
- record routed to dead letter;
- worker restarted;
- state restored;
- replay started;
- replay completed;
- backpressure activated;
- backpressure released;
- configuration changed;
- scaling action started;
- scaling action completed.

Implement distributed tracing across source ingestion, broker publication, queue consumption, operator execution, state access, checkpointing, sink delivery, retry, replay, and recovery. Propagate trace context through asynchronous boundaries, message headers, worker pools, retries, and dead-letter paths. Ensure every final sink result can be traced back to its source event and every source event can be traced through every operator, state transition, retry, checkpoint, and sink outcome.

Implement a metrics pipeline that accepts high-volume telemetry safely. Use bounded ingestion queues, batching, compression, backpressure, retry logic, dead-letter handling, idempotent writes, retention controls, and cardinality protection. Prevent metric labels from using unbounded values such as raw user identifiers, raw event identifiers, raw document identifiers, or arbitrary state keys. Store high-cardinality diagnostic events separately from low-cardinality time-series aggregates. Preserve enough sampled trace data to investigate tail latency and stateful correctness failures.

Implement a durable performance-event store for trace-level records, a time-series metrics store for aggregates, and a relational or analytical store for benchmark runs, configurations, experiment metadata, regression history, and service-level objective evaluations. Version all metric schemas. Support migrations. Support retention tiers:
- short retention for full-fidelity per-event diagnostics;
- medium retention for sampled traces and detailed histograms;
- long retention for aggregated latency, throughput, error, state-size, checkpoint, and service-level objective metrics.

Implement complete stateful streaming metrics. For every stateful operator, measure:
- active key count;
- state bytes;
- state entries;
- state bytes per key bucket;
- state read rate;
- state write rate;
- state mutation latency;
- state serialization and deserialization latency;
- state compaction duration;
- state eviction duration;
- state cleanup rate;
- state TTL expiration count;
- state spill count;
- disk spill bytes;
- memory pressure;
- garbage-collection or runtime pause contribution where available;
- checkpoint size;
- checkpoint duration;
- checkpoint upload or persistence rate;
- checkpoint failure count;
- checkpoint retry count;
- restore duration;
- replay duration;
- replay event count;
- state-store cache hit ratio;
- state-store cache miss ratio;
- state skew across partitions;
- largest state partition;
- median state partition;
- state rebalancing duration;
- state migration success and failure count.

Implement watermark metrics. For every source, partition, operator, and pipeline, record:
- maximum observed event time;
- current watermark;
- watermark advancement rate;
- watermark lag;
- allowed lateness configuration;
- count and rate of late events;
- count and rate of dropped late events;
- count and rate of redirected late events;
- window closure delay;
- join completion delay;
- state retained because the watermark has not advanced;
- state evicted after watermark progression;
- source partitions blocking watermark advancement;
- idle-source detection;
- out-of-order event distribution.

Watermarks are required to bound state in event-time stateful processing by defining a threshold after which old windows can be finalized and state can be evicted; measure their effect on both state size and result latency.[web:60][web:63] Treat watermark configuration as a correctness and latency trade-off: larger lateness allowances can improve completeness but delay result finalization and increase retained state, while smaller allowances can reduce latency and state size but increase late-data loss or rerouting.[web:60][web:63]

Implement checkpoint and recovery metrics. Stateful stream processing commonly combines replay with checkpointed operator state to resume consistently after failures; instrument both the checkpoint and replay sides of recovery.[web:57] Track checkpoint interval, alignment or coordination delay where applicable, state snapshot time, persistence time, checkpoint size, checkpoint queueing delay, checkpoint overlap, checkpoint-induced throughput drop, checkpoint-induced tail-latency increase, success rate, failure reasons, restore duration, replay duration, replay volume, duplicate prevention behavior, and end-to-end recovery time. Provide configuration validation that warns when checkpoint cadence, state size, storage throughput, or replay volume make recovery objectives unattainable.

Implement backpressure measurement. Measure source lag, broker lag, queue depth, queue age, queue arrival rate, queue service rate, worker utilization, worker saturation, consumer lag, retry rate, rejected work, dropped noncritical work, admission-control decisions, and load-shedding decisions. Add a clear backpressure state machine with inactive, rising, active, critical, recovering, and resolved states. Emit transition events and correlate them with latency, throughput, state growth, checkpointing, and resource metrics. Backpressure must be measured and controlled because unbounded producer-consumer imbalance causes growing queues, memory pressure, and rising latency.[web:43][web:26]

Implement workload generators that can create deterministic and configurable event streams. Support:
- steady-state constant-rate load;
- ramp-up load;
- burst load;
- periodic burst load;
- sawtooth load;
- randomized arrival distributions;
- hot-key skew;
- Zipf-distributed keys;
